{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mz6wkPvW99CHMMksmrbWyk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Adjust claims: (1) Characterization remains primary but ''domain\n2\t  heterogeneity'' downgraded to ''suggestive pattern requiring larger-scale confirmation''\n3\t  — present ranking/direction, not significance. (2) Signal failure claim narrowed\n4\t  to ''scale-dependent'' (0.5B specific). (3) Identifiability claim strengthened as\n5\t  explanation for WHY calibration doesn''t outperform linear — structural domain-method\n6\t  mismatch at N<50 is the real finding. (4) Calibration claim further demoted: IRT''s\n7\t  value is interpretable per-item decomposition and theoretical grounding, NOT better\n8\t  point estimates. Add honest ''When NOT to calibrate'' section as equal-weight contribution.\n9\t  Supplement with 1-2 additional negative results (GSM8K overcorrection, coverage\n10\t  collapse) framed as diagnostic insights.\n11\t\n12\t  '\n13\timagined_experiments:\n14\t- name: sft_d100_qc_only\n15\t  plan_id: exp-015\n16\t  description: '100% dosage SFT using only QC-pass paraphrases (7547/16697 items)\n17\t    on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Isolates noisy vs clean contamination confound. Compare calibration\n18\t    metrics against exp-001 d100 (all paraphrases) to quantify effect of noisy contamination\n19\t    on IRT estimates.\n20\t\n21\t    '\n22\t  expected_results:\n23\t    train_loss: 1.58\n24\t    token_accuracy: 0.67\n25\t    training_time_min: 55\n26\t    total_steps: 2800\n27\t    mmlu_accuracy: 0.258\n28\t    gsm8k_accuracy: 0.355\n29\t    arc_challenge_accuracy: 0.255\n30\t    humaneval_pass1: 0.073\n31\t    mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all: 0.025\n32\t    arc_delta_vs_d100_all: -0.039\n33\t    irt_gamma_qc_only: -0.95\n34\t    irt_gamma_qc_only_ci:\n35\t    - -2.1\n36\t    - 0.2\n37\t    irt_gamma_all_paraphrases: -1.21\n38\t    gamma_difference_significant: false\n39\t    mae_qc_only: 0.038\n40\t    mae_all_paraphrases: 0.035\n41\t    mae_gap_pp: 0.003\n42\t    coverage_95_qc_only: 0.35\n43\t    coverage_95_all: 0.33\n44\t  result_rationale: 'In the median tier, QC-only filtering produces modestly cleaner\n45\t    contamination signal (higher per-item semantic fidelity) but removes 55% of items,\n46\t    reducing total contamination volume. The net effect on 0.5B is near-neutral: the\n47\t    model''s limited capacity means it cannot fully exploit either the noisier-but-larger\n48\t    or cleaner-but-smaller contamination set. IRT γ estimate shifts slightly toward\n49\t    zero (less extreme forgetting) because QC-pass items have higher semantic similarity\n50\t    → less distribution shift → less catastrophic forgetting. However, the difference\n51\t    is NOT statistically significant (overlapping CIs) because N=8 models provides\n52\t    insufficient power to distinguish the two conditions. This supports the paper''s\n53\t    narrative that contamination quality modulates effect size but the IRT framework''s\n54\t    primary limitation is N, not signal noise. Calibration MAE remains comparable\n55\t    — the noise propagation finding (exp-005: CI insensitive to σ_e) already showed\n56\t    that exposure noise is not the binding constraint.\n57\t\n58\t    '\n59\t  tables:\n60\t  - name: 'Table: QC-only vs All-paraphrases comparison (0.5B, d100)'\n61\t    columns:\n62\t    - metric\n63\t    - qc_only\n64\t    - all_paraphrases\n65\t    - delta\n66\t    - significant\n67\t    rows:\n68\t    - - MMLU accuracy\n69\t      - 0.258\n70\t      - 0.233\n71\t      - 0.025\n72\t      - no (p=0.18)\n73\t    - - ARC accuracy\n74\t      - 0.255\n75\t      - 0.294\n76\t      - -0.039\n77\t      - no (p=0.22)\n78\t    - - GSM8K accuracy\n79\t      - 0.355\n80\t      - 0.365\n81\t      - -0.01\n82\t      - 'no'\n83\t    - - IRT γ (ARC)\n84\t      - -0.95\n85\t      - -1.21\n86\t      - 0.26\n87\t      - no (CI overlap)\n88\t    - - Calibrated MAE\n89\t      - 0.038\n90\t      - 0.035\n91\t      - 0.003\n92\t      - 'no'\n93\t    - - Coverage 95%\n94\t      - 0.35\n95\t      - 0.33\n96\t      - 0.02\n97\t      - 'no'\n98\t  figures_description:\n99\t  - Bar chart comparing γ estimates with 95% CIs for QC-only vs all-paraphrases conditions.\n100\t    Both CIs overlap substantially, showing non-significant difference.\n101\t  analysis: 'Result supports the identifiability-first narrative: the binding constraint\n102\t    on calibration quality is N_models (structural domain-method mismatch), not exposure\n103\t    signal noise. QC filtering improves signal quality but doesn''t materially change\n104\t    IRT outcomes at N=8. Paper uses this as supplementary evidence that the ''When\n105\t    NOT to calibrate'' guidance is driven by N limitations, not measurement error\n106\t    in Stage 1.\n107\t\n108\t    '\n109\t- name: scale_bridging_7b\n110\t  plan_id: exp-007\n111\t  description: 'Qwen2.5-7B controlled SFT injection at 5 dosage levels (0%, 5%, 25%,\n112\t    50%, 100%). Validates calibration accuracy scales to larger models. Multi-GPU\n113\t    node required.\n114\t\n115\t    '\n116\t  expected_results:\n117\t    training_hours_per_run: 6.5\n118\t    total_gpu_hours: 32.5\n119\t    d000_mmlu: 0.715\n120\t    d000_gsm8k: 0.785\n121\t    d000_arc: 0.885\n122\t    d000_humaneval: 0.28\n123\t    d005_mmlu: 0.716\n124\t    d005_gsm8k: 0.787\n125\t    d005_arc: 0.884\n126\t    d005_humaneval: 0.295\n127\t    d025_mmlu: 0.718\n128\t    d025_gsm8k: 0.79\n129\t    d025_arc: 0.883\n130\t    d025_humaneval: 0.31\n131\t    d100_mmlu: 0.722\n132\t    d100_gsm8k: 0.795\n133\t    d100_arc: 0.878\n134\t    d100_humaneval: 0.35\n135\t    mmlu_max_delta_pp: 0.7\n136\t    gsm8k_max_delta_pp: 1.0\n137\t    arc_max_delta_pp: -0.7\n138\t    humaneval_max_delta_pp: 7.0\n139\t    arc_gamma_7b_only: -0.15\n140\t    arc_gamma_7b_ci:\n141\t    - -0.8\n142\t    - 0.5\n143\t    arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n144\t    mmlu_gamma_7b_only: 0.08\n145\t    mmlu_gamma_7b_ci:\n146\t    - -0.6\n147\t    - 0.7\n148\t    s1_d005_auc_7b: 0.96\n149\t    s2_d005_auc_7b: 0.52\n150\t    s2_d005_cohens_d_7b: 0.08\n151\t    mae_calibrated_7b: 0.004\n152\t    mae_raw_7b: 0.003\n153\t    calibration_unnecessary: true\n154\t  result_rationale: 'In the median tier, 7B demonstrates near-immunity to MC benchmark\n155\t    contamination via SFT, confirming the scale-dependent characterization. The mechanism:\n156\t    7B has sufficient capacity that SFT contamination occupies a negligible fraction\n157\t    of model parameters — no catastrophic forgetting (unlike 0.5B) and no detectable\n158\t    inflation on MC benchmarks (unlike the pattern at smaller scales). ARC shows a\n159\t    tiny negative trend (-0.7pp at d100) but this is within noise. HumanEval shows\n160\t    the only meaningful dose-response (+7pp), consistent with 1.5B findings where\n161\t    code generation was the only benchmark affected at larger scales.\n162\t\n163\t    IRT γ at 7B alone is effectively zero with wide CI — contamination effect is undetectable\n164\t    at this scale. This is the \"near-immunity\" regime in the three-regime taxonomy.\n165\t    The critical implication: calibration is unnecessary at 7B (raw MAE already <\n166\t    0.5pp), and IRT correctly identifies this via non-significant γ. This validates\n167\t    the calibration gating criterion: when γ CI includes zero, report raw scores.\n168\t\n169\t    S2 (Min-K++) at 7B shows d≈0 (neither reversed nor effective) — distinct from\n170\t    the directional reversal at 0.5B. This narrows the \"signal failure\" claim to be\n171\t    explicitly scale-dependent: reversal at 0.5B, ineffectiveness at 3B/7B, unknown\n172\t    at larger scales. The reduced statistical strength in median tier means we cannot\n173\t    claim a clean monotonic relationship between scale and γ magnitude — just the\n174\t    directional pattern.\n175\t\n176\t    '\n177\t  tables:\n178\t  - name: 'Table: Contamination effect by scale (ARC-Challenge)'\n179\t    columns:\n180\t    - scale\n181\t    - d000_acc\n182\t    - d100_acc\n183\t    - delta_pp\n184\t    - gamma\n185\t    - gamma_ci_excludes_zero\n186\t    rows:\n187\t    - - 0.5B\n188\t      - 0.47\n189\t      - 0.294\n190\t      - -17.6\n191\t      - -1.21\n192\t      - borderline (0.12)\n193\t    - - 1.5B\n194\t      - 0.772\n195\t      - 0.769\n196\t      - -0.3\n197\t      - -0.18\n198\t      - 'no'\n199\t    - - 3B\n200\t      - 0.828\n201\t      - 0.84\n202\t      - 1.2\n203\t      - -0.42\n204\t      - 'no'\n205\t    - - 7B\n206\t      - 0.885\n207\t      - 0.878\n208\t      - -0.7\n209\t      - -0.15\n210\t      - 'no'\n211\t  - name: 'Table: Three-regime taxonomy evidence'\n212\t    columns:\n213\t    - regime\n214\t    - scale\n215\t    - arc_delta_pp\n216\t    - mechanism\n217\t    - gamma_significant\n218\t    rows:\n219\t    - - Catastrophic forgetting\n220\t      - 0.5B\n221\t      - -17.6\n222\t      - Capacity displacement\n223\t      - borderline\n224\t    - - Mild perturbation\n225\t      - 1.5B-3B\n226\t      - -0.3 to +1.2\n227\t      - Absorbed without displacement\n228\t      - 'no'\n229\t    - - Near-immunity\n230\t      - 7B\n231\t      - -0.7\n232\t      - Negligible relative to capacity\n233\t      - 'no'\n234\t  figures_description:\n235\t  - 'Line plot: contamination dosage (x) vs accuracy delta from d000 (y), one line\n236\t    per scale (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B). Shows 0.5B steep negative slope, others flat. ARC-Challenge.'\n237\t  - 'Heatmap: scale × dosage → γ magnitude, showing attenuation with scale.'\n238\t  analysis: '7B data strengthens the scale-dependent characterization but weakens\n239\t    the overall statistical case for γ as a useful calibration parameter. At 7B, γ≈0\n240\t    means ContamCal correctly identifies ''no calibration needed'' — this is actually\n241\t    a success of the gating criterion, not a failure. Paper narrative: ContamCal''s\n242\t    value at large scales is diagnostic (confirming no contamination effect) rather\n243\t    than corrective. The three-regime taxonomy holds directionally but only the 0.5B\n244\t    regime has borderline-significant γ when fitted alone — the pooled N=24 analysis\n245\t    is needed for statistical power.\n246\t\n247\t    '\n248\t- name: multi_family_validation\n249\t  plan_id: exp-014\n250\t  description: 'Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-1B at 3 dosage levels (0%, 25%,\n251\t    100%). Validates that identifiability boundaries and calibration accuracy generalize\n252\t    beyond Qwen2.5. Addresses single model family limitation.\n253\t\n254\t    '\n255\t  expected_results:\n256\t    training_hours_per_run: 3.5\n257\t    total_gpu_hours: 10.5\n258\t    d000_mmlu: 0.465\n259\t    d000_gsm8k: 0.42\n260\t    d000_arc: 0.625\n261\t    d000_humaneval: 0.095\n262\t    d025_mmlu: 0.46\n263\t    d025_gsm8k: 0.425\n264\t    d025_arc: 0.61\n265\t    d025_humaneval: 0.11\n266\t    d100_mmlu: 0.445\n267\t    d100_gsm8k: 0.435\n268\t    d100_arc: 0.57\n269\t    d100_humaneval: 0.125\n270\t    arc_delta_d100_pp: -5.5\n271\t    mmlu_delta_d100_pp: -2.0\n272\t    gsm8k_delta_d100_pp: 1.5\n273\t    llama_vs_qwen_susceptibility_ratio: 0.31\n274\t    arc_gamma_llama: -0.75\n275\t    arc_gamma_llama_ci:\n276\t    - -1.8\n277\t    - 0.3\n278\t    arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n279\t    gamma_sign_consistent_arc: true\n280\t    gamma_sign_consistent_gsm8k: true\n281\t    gamma_magnitude_ratio_llama_vs_qwen: 0.55\n282\t    multi_family_gamma_rank_tau: 0.6\n283\t    s1_d005_auc_llama: 0.94\n284\t    s2_d005_auc_llama: 0.48\n285\t    s2_d100_cohens_d_llama: -0.85\n286\t  result_rationale: 'In the median tier, Llama-3.2-1B shows the same directional pattern\n287\t    as Qwen (ARC forgetting, GSM8K mild inflation) but with substantially reduced\n288\t    magnitude. The γ sign is consistent (negative for ARC) but magnitude is roughly\n289\t    half that of Qwen at comparable scale. This is expected: Llama-3.2-1B has different\n290\t    architecture (GQA, different tokenizer, different pre-training data distribution)\n291\t    which affects how SFT contamination interacts with stored knowledge. The rank\n292\t    correlation (τ=0.60) is moderate — consistent direction but different magnitude\n293\t    means the benchmarks don''t perfectly rank-order in susceptibility across families.\n294\t\n295\t    Critically, the γ CI for Llama alone does NOT exclude zero — this is the reduced\n296\t    statistical strength characteristic of median tier. Only when pooled with Qwen\n297\t    data (raising N toward 24) does the combined γ become borderline significant.\n298\t    This means the multi-family finding is ''directionally consistent'' but not independently\n299\t    confirmatory at the individual-family level.\n300\t\n301\t    S2 (Min-K++) shows a weaker reversal on Llama (d=-0.85 vs d=-1.98 on Qwen at 0.5B),\n302\t    still negative but less extreme. This is consistent with the ''scale-dependent''\n303\t    signal failure narrative: Llama-3.2-1B at 1B parameters is between 0.5B and 1.5B\n304\t    Qwen in terms of effective capacity, so the reversal is attenuated. S1 remains\n305\t    robust (AUC=0.94).\n306\t\n307\t    '\n308\t  tables:\n309\t  - name: 'Table: Cross-family contamination susceptibility'\n310\t    columns:\n311\t    - family\n312\t    - scale\n313\t    - arc_d100_delta_pp\n314\t    - arc_gamma\n315\t    - gamma_sign\n316\t    - ci_excludes_zero\n317\t    rows:\n318\t    - - Qwen2.5\n319\t      - 0.5B\n320\t      - -17.6\n321\t      - -1.21\n322\t      - negative\n323\t      - borderline\n324\t    - - Qwen2.5\n325\t      - 1.5B\n326\t      - -0.3\n327\t      - -0.18\n328\t      - negative\n329\t      - 'no'\n330\t    - - Qwen2.5\n331\t      - 3B\n332\t      - 1.2\n333\t      - -0.42\n334\t      - negative\n335\t      - 'no'\n336\t    - - Llama-3.2\n337\t      - 1B\n338\t      - -5.5\n339\t      - -0.75\n340\t      - negative\n341\t      - 'no'\n342\t  - name: 'Table: Signal effectiveness cross-family'\n343\t    columns:\n344\t    - signal\n345\t    - metric\n346\t    - Qwen_0.5B\n347\t    - Llama_1B\n348\t    - consistent\n349\t    rows:\n350\t    - - S1 embedding\n351\t      - d005 AUC\n352\t      - 0.979\n353\t      - 0.94\n354\t      - 'yes'\n355\t    - - S2 Min-K++\n356\t      - d005 AUC\n357\t      - 0.465\n358\t      - 0.48\n359\t      - yes (both fail)\n360\t    - - S2 Min-K++\n361\t      - d100 Cohen's d\n362\t      - -1.98\n363\t      - -0.85\n364\t      - yes (both negative)\n365\t    - - S4 Self-Critique\n366\t      - d005 AUC\n367\t      - 0.481\n368\t      - 0.5\n369\t      - yes (both fail)\n370\t  figures_description:\n371\t  - 'Grouped bar chart: ARC accuracy delta (d100-d000) by model family and scale.\n372\t    Shows Llama less susceptible than Qwen at comparable scale.'\n373\t  - 'Scatter plot: Qwen γ_k vs Llama γ_k by benchmark, with identity line. Points\n374\t    cluster in same quadrant but not on diagonal (consistent sign, different magnitude).'\n375\t  analysis: 'Multi-family result confirms contamination susceptibility is partially\n376\t    a benchmark property (ARC consistently shows forgetting) but magnitude is model-family-dependent.\n377\t    This weakens the strongest form of the characterization claim (''contamination\n378\t    is a benchmark property'') to the softer ''contamination direction is benchmark-determined\n379\t    but magnitude is family-modulated.'' Kendall τ=0.60 provides moderate support.\n380\t    The paper presents this honestly: ''directional consistency across families with\n381\t    heterogeneous magnitude suggests contamination characterization requires per-family\n382\t    calibration for quantitative use.''\n383\t\n384\t    '\n385\t- name: rl_observational_case_study\n386\t  plan_id: exp-009\n387\t  description: 'Apply Self-Critique + available signals to existing RL-trained models:\n388\t    Qwen2.5-Instruct (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B/7B. Fit IRT with\n389\t    wider CIs. Expected regret quantification for model selection. Near-zero γ check\n390\t    on known-clean benchmark (specificity test).\n391\t\n392\t    '\n393\t  expected_results:\n394\t    n_models_evaluated: 8\n395\t    s1_computable: true\n396\t    s2_computable: true\n397\t    s4_computable: true\n398\t    s1_auc_instruct_d005_equivalent: 0.72\n399\t    s2_instruct_mean_score: 1.69\n400\t    s4_instruct_mean_score: -0.91\n401\t    arc_gamma_observational: -0.52\n402\t    arc_gamma_observational_ci:\n403\t    - -1.8\n404\t    - 0.8\n405\t    arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n406\t    mmlu_gamma_observational: 0.18\n407\t    mmlu_gamma_observational_ci:\n408\t    - -0.9\n409\t    - 1.3\n410\t    gsm8k_gamma_observational: 0.35\n411\t    gsm8k_gamma_observational_ci:\n412\t    - -0.6\n413\t    - 1.3\n414\t    specificity_gamma: 0.05\n415\t    specificity_gamma_ci:\n416\t    - -0.7\n417\t    - 0.8\n418\t    specificity_passes: true\n419\t    mean_ci_width_observational: 0.12\n420\t    mean_ci_width_controlled: 0.07\n421\t    ci_ratio: 1.7\n422\t    regret_raw_top5: 0.015\n423\t    regret_calibrated_top5: 0.018\n424\t    regret_remove_rescore_top5: 0.012\n425\t    calibration_improves_selection: false\n426\t    deepseek_r1_arc_gamma_contribution: -0.3\n427\t    deepseek_s1_score_elevated: true\n428\t  result_rationale: 'In the median tier, the observational case study produces directional\n429\t    but not statistically significant findings. Without ground-truth contamination\n430\t    labels, the IRT fit relies entirely on Stage 1 signal scores as exposure proxies.\n431\t    S1 (embedding similarity) on Instruct models is noisier than on controlled SFT\n432\t    models because RL post-training alters the relationship between training data\n433\t    exposure and embedding space proximity.\n434\t\n435\t    ARC γ is negative (consistent with controlled findings) but CI widely includes\n436\t    zero — the observational setting lacks the statistical power of the controlled\n437\t    experiment. This is expected: without dosage variation, all models cluster near\n438\t    similar exposure levels, collapsing the range that IRT needs to estimate γ reliably.\n439\t\n440\t    Expected regret analysis shows calibration does NOT improve model selection in\n441\t    this setting. Raw scores already rank models reasonably well (because contamination\n442\t    effects at 1.5B+ are small per the scale-dependent finding). Remove-and-rescore\n443\t    performs marginally better because it simply drops high-exposure items without\n444\t    the risk of IRT miscalibration. This is an honest negative result that reinforces\n445\t    the ''When NOT to calibrate'' message: in observational settings with no dosage\n446\t    variation and large models, calibration overhead is not justified.\n447\t\n448\t    Specificity check passes: γ≈0 on TriviaQA (a benchmark not in training data) confirms\n449\t    the framework doesn''t hallucinate contamination effects where none exist. This\n450\t    is a valuable positive control.\n451\t\n452\t    '\n453\t  tables:\n454\t  - name: 'Table: Observational γ estimates (Instruct + DeepSeek models)'\n455\t    columns:\n456\t    - benchmark\n457\t    - gamma\n458\t    - ci_lower\n459\t    - ci_upper\n460\t    - ci_excludes_zero\n461\t    - interpretation\n462\t    rows:\n463\t    - - ARC-Challenge\n464\t      - -0.52\n465\t      - -1.8\n466\t      - 0.8\n467\t      - 'no'\n468\t      - Directionally consistent with controlled (forgetting), underpowered\n469\t    - - MMLU\n470\t      - 0.18\n471\t      - -0.9\n472\t      - 1.3\n473\t      - 'no'\n474\t      - Near-zero, consistent with scale-dependent attenuation\n475\t    - - GSM8K\n476\t      - 0.35\n477\t      - -0.6\n478\t      - 1.3\n479\t      - 'no'\n480\t      - Non-significant, consistent with gating rejection\n481\t    - - TriviaQA (specificity)\n482\t      - 0.05\n483\t      - -0.7\n484\t      - 0.8\n485\t      - 'no'\n486\t      - PASS — no false positive\n487\t  - name: 'Table: Expected regret — top-5 model selection'\n488\t    columns:\n489\t    - method\n490\t    - regret_pp\n491\t    - rank_tau_vs_clean\n492\t    - ci_width\n493\t    rows:\n494\t    - - Raw scores\n495\t      - 1.5\n496\t      - 0.85\n497\t      - N/A\n498\t    - - ContamCal calibrated\n499\t      - 1.8\n500\t      - 0.8\n501\t      - 0.12\n502\t    - - Remove-and-rescore\n503\t      - 1.2\n504\t      - 0.88\n505\t      - N/A\n506\t    - - Linear regression\n507\t      - 1.6\n508\t      - 0.83\n509\t      - 0.08\n510\t  figures_description:\n511\t  - 'Forest plot: γ estimates with 95% CIs for each benchmark in observational setting,\n512\t    with controlled setting estimates overlaid as reference. Shows consistency in\n513\t    direction but wider CIs.'\n514\t  - 'Scatter: raw model rank vs calibrated model rank (top-5 highlighted), showing\n515\t    minimal rank changes — calibration doesn''t improve selection.'\n516\t  analysis: 'The observational case study is an honest demonstration of ContamCal''s\n517\t    limitations in the wild. Without controlled dosage variation, the framework correctly\n518\t    identifies directional effects but lacks power for quantitative calibration. This\n519\t    supports the paper''s ''When NOT to calibrate'' section: observational settings\n520\t    with large models (≥1.5B) and no dosage variation are below the identifiability\n521\t    threshold. The specificity check is the key positive finding — validating that\n522\t    the framework doesn''t produce false contamination diagnoses. Paper frames this\n523\t    as ''ContamCal as diagnostic tool (is there contamination?) rather than corrective\n524\t    tool (what''s the true score?) in observational settings.''\n525\t\n526\t    '\n527\t- name: observational_72b\n528\t  plan_id: exp-012\n529\t  description: 'Qwen2.5-72B observational analysis (inference only, no SFT). Extends\n530\t    ecological validity to frontier model scale.\n531\t\n532\t    '\n533\t  expected_results:\n534\t    inference_gpu_hours: 12\n535\t    mmlu_accuracy: 0.842\n536\t    gsm8k_accuracy: 0.905\n537\t    arc_challenge_accuracy: 0.945\n538\t    humaneval_pass1: 0.685\n539\t    s1_mean_exposure_mmlu: 0.42\n540\t    s1_mean_exposure_arc: 0.38\n541\t    s1_mean_exposure_gsm8k: 0.35\n542\t    s2_minkpp_mean: 1.72\n543\t    s4_selfcritique_mean: -0.88\n544\t    contribution_to_pooled_n: 1\n545\t    pooled_n_after_72b: 9\n546\t    s1_72b_vs_3b_delta: 0.05\n547\t    s1_72b_relative_rank: middle (not highest, not lowest)\n548\t    position_bias_72b_mmlu: 0.02\n549\t    position_bias_72b_arc: 0.015\n550\t    position_bias_absent: true\n551\t  result_rationale: 'In the median tier, 72B provides ecological validity but minimal\n552\t    statistical contribution. A single model cannot independently estimate γ — it\n553\t    only adds one data point to the pooled analysis. The 72B model''s near-ceiling\n554\t    performance on ARC (94.5%) and strong performance on MMLU (84.2%) means contamination\n555\t    effects, if any, are masked by the model''s genuine capability. This is the ''near-immunity''\n556\t    regime taken to its extreme.\n557\t\n558\t    S1 exposure scores are moderate (not zero, because 72B''s training data likely\n559\t    includes benchmark-adjacent content given its massive training corpus), but this\n560\t    doesn''t imply contamination harm — it''s the expected background exposure level\n561\t    for a frontier model. The key contribution is confirming that position bias (which\n562\t    invalidated 0.5B MC data) is absent at 72B, validating that logprob-based MC evaluation\n563\t    is reliable at frontier scale.\n564\t\n565\t    The finding supports the scale-dependent narrative without adding statistical\n566\t    power. Paper uses 72B as a single sentence: ''Contamination effect at frontier\n567\t    scale (72B) is undetectable, consistent with the three-regime taxonomy.''\n568\t\n569\t    '\n570\t  tables:\n571\t  - name: 'Table: 72B observational results'\n572\t    columns:\n573\t    - benchmark\n574\t    - accuracy\n575\t    - s1_mean_exposure\n576\t    - interpretation\n577\t    rows:\n578\t    - - MMLU\n579\t      - 0.842\n580\t      - 0.42\n581\t      - Strong performance, moderate background exposure\n582\t    - - ARC-Challenge\n583\t      - 0.945\n584\t      - 0.38\n585\t      - Near-ceiling, exposure ≠ inflation\n586\t    - - GSM8K\n587\t      - 0.905\n588\t      - 0.35\n589\t      - Strong, below ARC/MMLU exposure\n590\t    - - HumanEval\n591\t      - 0.685\n592\t      - 0.28\n593\t      - Lowest exposure (code is harder to memorize)\n594\t  figures_description:\n595\t  - Single point added to the scale vs contamination-effect plot (Figure 2 from exp-007).\n596\t    72B sits firmly in the 'near-immunity' plateau.\n597\t  analysis: '72B is appendix-level evidence. Its primary value is completeness (showing\n598\t    the framework applies at frontier scale) and the position bias absence confirmation.\n599\t    Does not change any statistical conclusions. In median tier, this experiment is\n600\t    ''included for ecological validity'' — one sentence in the main text, details\n601\t    in appendix.\n602\t\n603\t    '\n604\t- name: closed_weight_2signal_mode\n605\t  plan_id: exp-013\n606\t  description: 'Degraded 2-signal mode (ConStat + Self-Critique, no logits/embeddings)\n607\t    for closed-weight models. Ablation quantifying 2-signal vs 4-signal calibration\n608\t    accuracy gap.\n609\t\n610\t    '\n611\t  expected_results:\n612\t    n_signals: 2\n613\t    s3_constat_d005_auc: 0.55\n614\t    s4_selfcritique_d005_auc: 0.48\n615\t    rrf_2signal_d005_auc: 0.56\n616\t    rrf_4signal_d005_auc: 0.97\n617\t    auc_degradation: 0.41\n618\t    arc_gamma_2signal: -0.35\n619\t    arc_gamma_2signal_ci:\n620\t    - -2.5\n621\t    - 1.8\n622\t    arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n623\t    mae_2signal: 0.065\n624\t    mae_4signal: 0.035\n625\t    mae_raw: 0.059\n626\t    coverage_95_2signal: 0.25\n627\t    coverage_95_4signal: 0.35\n628\t    calibration_improves_over_raw: false\n629\t    mae_gap_2signal_vs_raw: 0.006\n630\t    kendall_tau_2signal: 0.55\n631\t    kendall_tau_4signal: 0.76\n632\t  result_rationale: 'In the median tier, the 2-signal mode is essentially non-functional\n633\t    for calibration. Both ConStat and Self-Critique showed AUC≈0.5 (chance level)\n634\t    at 0.5B in exp-008. Without S1 (the only effective signal), the exposure scores\n635\t    fed to IRT are near-random, making γ estimation impossible. The resulting γ CI\n636\t    is extremely wide and includes zero — IRT cannot distinguish contaminated from\n637\t    clean items when the input signal is noise.\n638\t\n639\t    Calibration MAE is WORSE than raw (0.065 vs 0.059) because the noisy exposure\n640\t    scores introduce systematic error: IRT attempts to ''correct'' based on meaningless\n641\t    signal variation, producing biased counterfactual estimates. This is the calibration\n642\t    gating criterion in action: when input signal AUC < 0.55 (per exp-004 simulation),\n643\t    calibration should not be attempted.\n644\t\n645\t    This result is presented honestly as a hard limitation: ContamCal requires model\n646\t    weights (for S1 embedding similarity) to function. Closed-weight evaluation is\n647\t    restricted to ConStat''s performance gap approach, which requires access to both\n648\t    original and paraphrased items but not model internals — however, this alone is\n649\t    insufficient for reliable calibration. Paper frames: ''closed-weight calibration\n650\t    remains an open problem; ContamCal''s requirement for embedding access is a genuine\n651\t    limitation, not a design choice that could be easily relaxed.''\n652\t\n653\t    '\n654\t  tables:\n655\t  - name: 'Table: 2-signal vs 4-signal calibration comparison'\n656\t    columns:\n657\t    - metric\n658\t    - 2_signal\n659\t    - 4_signal\n660\t    - raw\n661\t    - winner\n662\t    rows:\n663\t    - - Composite AUC\n664\t      - 0.56\n665\t      - 0.97\n666\t      - N/A\n667\t      - 4-signal\n668\t    - - γ CI width\n669\t      - 4.3\n670\t      - 2.65\n671\t      - N/A\n672\t      - 4-signal\n673\t    - - γ excludes zero\n674\t      - 'no'\n675\t      - borderline\n676\t      - N/A\n677\t      - 4-signal\n678\t    - - MAE\n679\t      - 0.065\n680\t      - 0.035\n681\t      - 0.059\n682\t      - 4-signal\n683\t    - - Coverage 95%\n684\t      - 0.25\n685\t      - 0.35\n686\t      - N/A\n687\t      - 4-signal\n688\t    - - Kendall τ\n689\t      - 0.55\n690\t      - 0.76\n691\t      - 0.82\n692\t      - raw\n693\t  - name: 'Table: Signal AUC by mode'\n694\t    columns:\n695\t    - signal\n696\t    - available_closed_weight\n697\t    - d005_auc\n698\t    - contribution\n699\t    rows:\n700\t    - - S1 embedding\n701\t      - no (requires weights)\n702\t      - 0.979\n703\t      - dominant\n704\t    - - S2 Min-K++\n705\t      - no (requires logits)\n706\t      - 0.465\n707\t      - none (chance)\n708\t    - - S3 ConStat\n709\t      - 'yes'\n710\t      - 0.55\n711\t      - marginal\n712\t    - - S4 Self-Critique\n713\t      - 'yes'\n714\t      - 0.48\n715\t      - none (chance)\n716\t  figures_description:\n717\t  - 'Side-by-side calibration scatter plots: 2-signal (left, high scatter, regression\n718\t    line far from identity) vs 4-signal (right, tighter, closer to identity).'\n719\t  - ROC curves for 2-signal RRF vs 4-signal RRF, showing massive AUC gap.\n720\t  analysis: 'The 2-signal mode is a confirmed failure mode that the paper presents\n721\t    transparently. This strengthens the honest ''limitations'' section and supports\n722\t    the broader narrative: contamination calibration fundamentally requires access\n723\t    to model internals (at minimum, embedding space) because surface-level signals\n724\t    (performance gaps, entropy patterns) lack discriminative power at the item level.\n725\t    In median tier, this negative result is not surprising — it follows directly from\n726\t    exp-008 showing S3/S4 at chance — but it''s the formal ablation needed for the\n727\t    paper. Contributes to the ''When NOT to calibrate'' section: ''Do not attempt\n728\t    calibration when (a) only closed-weight signals available, (b) composite signal\n729\t    AUC < 0.60.'''\n730\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"20047d13-ff57-4eae-9259-56509c0ed9db","uuid":"3912f0d2-0a45-483d-917e-d916087ed2a2","timestamp":"2026-05-16T11:28:33.480Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T104822/median/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Adjust claims: (1) Characterization remains primary but ''domain\n  heterogeneity'' downgraded to ''suggestive pattern requiring larger-scale confirmation''\n  — present ranking/direction, not significance. (2) Signal failure claim narrowed\n  to ''scale-dependent'' (0.5B specific). (3) Identifiability claim strengthened as\n  explanation for WHY calibration doesn''t outperform linear — structural domain-method\n  mismatch at N<50 is the real finding. (4) Calibration claim further demoted: IRT''s\n  value is interpretable per-item decomposition and theoretical grounding, NOT better\n  point estimates. Add honest ''When NOT to calibrate'' section as equal-weight contribution.\n  Supplement with 1-2 additional negative results (GSM8K overcorrection, coverage\n  collapse) framed as diagnostic insights.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: sft_d100_qc_only\n  plan_id: exp-015\n  description: '100% dosage SFT using only QC-pass paraphrases (7547/16697 items)\n    on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Isolates noisy vs clean contamination confound. Compare calibration\n    metrics against exp-001 d100 (all paraphrases) to quantify effect of noisy contamination\n    on IRT estimates.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    train_loss: 1.58\n    token_accuracy: 0.67\n    training_time_min: 55\n    total_steps: 2800\n    mmlu_accuracy: 0.258\n    gsm8k_accuracy: 0.355\n    arc_challenge_accuracy: 0.255\n    humaneval_pass1: 0.073\n    mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all: 0.025\n    arc_delta_vs_d100_all: -0.039\n    irt_gamma_qc_only: -0.95\n    irt_gamma_qc_only_ci:\n    - -2.1\n    - 0.2\n    irt_gamma_all_paraphrases: -1.21\n    gamma_difference_significant: false\n    mae_qc_only: 0.038\n    mae_all_paraphrases: 0.035\n    mae_gap_pp: 0.003\n    coverage_95_qc_only: 0.35\n    coverage_95_all: 0.33\n  result_rationale: 'In the median tier, QC-only filtering produces modestly cleaner\n    contamination signal (higher per-item semantic fidelity) but removes 55% of items,\n    reducing total contamination volume. The net effect on 0.5B is near-neutral: the\n    model''s limited capacity means it cannot fully exploit either the noisier-but-larger\n    or cleaner-but-smaller contamination set. IRT γ estimate shifts slightly toward\n    zero (less extreme forgetting) because QC-pass items have higher semantic similarity\n    → less distribution shift → less catastrophic forgetting. However, the difference\n    is NOT statistically significant (overlapping CIs) because N=8 models provides\n    insufficient power to distinguish the two conditions. This supports the paper''s\n    narrative that contamination quality modulates effect size but the IRT framework''s\n    primary limitation is N, not signal noise. Calibration MAE remains comparable\n    — the noise propagation finding (exp-005: CI insensitive to σ_e) already showed\n    that exposure noise is not the binding constraint.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: QC-only vs All-paraphrases comparison (0.5B, d100)'\n    columns:\n    - metric\n    - qc_only\n    - all_paraphrases\n    - delta\n    - significant\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU accuracy\n      - 0.258\n      - 0.233\n      - 0.025\n      - no (p=0.18)\n    - - ARC accuracy\n      - 0.255\n      - 0.294\n      - -0.039\n      - no (p=0.22)\n    - - GSM8K accuracy\n      - 0.355\n      - 0.365\n      - -0.01\n      - 'no'\n    - - IRT γ (ARC)\n      - -0.95\n      - -1.21\n      - 0.26\n      - no (CI overlap)\n    - - Calibrated MAE\n      - 0.038\n      - 0.035\n      - 0.003\n      - 'no'\n    - - Coverage 95%\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.02\n      - 'no'\n  figures_description:\n  - Bar chart comparing γ estimates with 95% CIs for QC-only vs all-paraphrases conditions.\n    Both CIs overlap substantially, showing non-significant difference.\n  analysis: 'Result supports the identifiability-first narrative: the binding constraint\n    on calibration quality is N_models (structural domain-method mismatch), not exposure\n    signal noise. QC filtering improves signal quality but doesn''t materially change\n    IRT outcomes at N=8. Paper uses this as supplementary evidence that the ''When\n    NOT to calibrate'' guidance is driven by N limitations, not measurement error\n    in Stage 1.\n\n    '\n- name: scale_bridging_7b\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: 'Qwen2.5-7B controlled SFT injection at 5 dosage levels (0%, 5%, 25%,\n    50%, 100%). Validates calibration accuracy scales to larger models. Multi-GPU\n    node required.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    training_hours_per_run: 6.5\n    total_gpu_hours: 32.5\n    d000_mmlu: 0.715\n    d000_gsm8k: 0.785\n    d000_arc: 0.885\n    d000_humaneval: 0.28\n    d005_mmlu: 0.716\n    d005_gsm8k: 0.787\n    d005_arc: 0.884\n    d005_humaneval: 0.295\n    d025_mmlu: 0.718\n    d025_gsm8k: 0.79\n    d025_arc: 0.883\n    d025_humaneval: 0.31\n    d100_mmlu: 0.722\n    d100_gsm8k: 0.795\n    d100_arc: 0.878\n    d100_humaneval: 0.35\n    mmlu_max_delta_pp: 0.7\n    gsm8k_max_delta_pp: 1.0\n    arc_max_delta_pp: -0.7\n    humaneval_max_delta_pp: 7.0\n    arc_gamma_7b_only: -0.15\n    arc_gamma_7b_ci:\n    - -0.8\n    - 0.5\n    arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    mmlu_gamma_7b_only: 0.08\n    mmlu_gamma_7b_ci:\n    - -0.6\n    - 0.7\n    s1_d005_auc_7b: 0.96\n    s2_d005_auc_7b: 0.52\n    s2_d005_cohens_d_7b: 0.08\n    mae_calibrated_7b: 0.004\n    mae_raw_7b: 0.003\n    calibration_unnecessary: true\n  result_rationale: 'In the median tier, 7B demonstrates near-immunity to MC benchmark\n    contamination via SFT, confirming the scale-dependent characterization. The mechanism:\n    7B has sufficient capacity that SFT contamination occupies a negligible fraction\n    of model parameters — no catastrophic forgetting (unlike 0.5B) and no detectable\n    inflation on MC benchmarks (unlike the pattern at smaller scales). ARC shows a\n    tiny negative trend (-0.7pp at d100) but this is within noise. HumanEval shows\n    the only meaningful dose-response (+7pp), consistent with 1.5B findings where\n    code generation was the only benchmark affected at larger scales.\n\n    IRT γ at 7B alone is effectively zero with wide CI — contamination effect is undetectable\n    at this scale. This is the \"near-immunity\" regime in the three-regime taxonomy.\n    The critical implication: calibration is unnecessary at 7B (raw MAE already <\n    0.5pp), and IRT correctly identifies this via non-significant γ. This validates\n    the calibration gating criterion: when γ CI includes zero, report raw scores.\n\n    S2 (Min-K++) at 7B shows d≈0 (neither reversed nor effective) — distinct from\n    the directional reversal at 0.5B. This narrows the \"signal failure\" claim to be\n    explicitly scale-dependent: reversal at 0.5B, ineffectiveness at 3B/7B, unknown\n    at larger scales. The reduced statistical strength in median tier means we cannot\n    claim a clean monotonic relationship between scale and γ magnitude — just the\n    directional pattern.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Contamination effect by scale (ARC-Challenge)'\n    columns:\n    - scale\n    - d000_acc\n    - d100_acc\n    - delta_pp\n    - gamma\n    - gamma_ci_excludes_zero\n    rows:\n    - - 0.5B\n      - 0.47\n      - 0.294\n      - -17.6\n      - -1.21\n      - borderline (0.12)\n    - - 1.5B\n      - 0.772\n      - 0.769\n      - -0.3\n      - -0.18\n      - 'no'\n    - - 3B\n      - 0.828\n      - 0.84\n      - 1.2\n      - -0.42\n      - 'no'\n    - - 7B\n      - 0.885\n      - 0.878\n      - -0.7\n      - -0.15\n      - 'no'\n  - name: 'Table: Three-regime taxonomy evidence'\n    columns:\n    - regime\n    - scale\n    - arc_delta_pp\n    - mechanism\n    - gamma_significant\n    rows:\n    - - Catastrophic forgetting\n      - 0.5B\n      - -17.6\n      - Capacity displacement\n      - borderline\n    - - Mild perturbation\n      - 1.5B-3B\n      - -0.3 to +1.2\n      - Absorbed without displacement\n      - 'no'\n    - - Near-immunity\n      - 7B\n      - -0.7\n      - Negligible relative to capacity\n      - 'no'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: contamination dosage (x) vs accuracy delta from d000 (y), one line\n    per scale (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B). Shows 0.5B steep negative slope, others flat. ARC-Challenge.'\n  - 'Heatmap: scale × dosage → γ magnitude, showing attenuation with scale.'\n  analysis: '7B data strengthens the scale-dependent characterization but weakens\n    the overall statistical case for γ as a useful calibration parameter. At 7B, γ≈0\n    means ContamCal correctly identifies ''no calibration needed'' — this is actually\n    a success of the gating criterion, not a failure. Paper narrative: ContamCal''s\n    value at large scales is diagnostic (confirming no contamination effect) rather\n    than corrective. The three-regime taxonomy holds directionally but only the 0.5B\n    regime has borderline-significant γ when fitted alone — the pooled N=24 analysis\n    is needed for statistical power.\n\n    '\n- name: multi_family_validation\n  plan_id: exp-014\n  description: 'Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-1B at 3 dosage levels (0%, 25%,\n    100%). Validates that identifiability boundaries and calibration accuracy generalize\n    beyond Qwen2.5. Addresses single model family limitation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    training_hours_per_run: 3.5\n    total_gpu_hours: 10.5\n    d000_mmlu: 0.465\n    d000_gsm8k: 0.42\n    d000_arc: 0.625\n    d000_humaneval: 0.095\n    d025_mmlu: 0.46\n    d025_gsm8k: 0.425\n    d025_arc: 0.61\n    d025_humaneval: 0.11\n    d100_mmlu: 0.445\n    d100_gsm8k: 0.435\n    d100_arc: 0.57\n    d100_humaneval: 0.125\n    arc_delta_d100_pp: -5.5\n    mmlu_delta_d100_pp: -2.0\n    gsm8k_delta_d100_pp: 1.5\n    llama_vs_qwen_susceptibility_ratio: 0.31\n    arc_gamma_llama: -0.75\n    arc_gamma_llama_ci:\n    - -1.8\n    - 0.3\n    arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    gamma_sign_consistent_arc: true\n    gamma_sign_consistent_gsm8k: true\n    gamma_magnitude_ratio_llama_vs_qwen: 0.55\n    multi_family_gamma_rank_tau: 0.6\n    s1_d005_auc_llama: 0.94\n    s2_d005_auc_llama: 0.48\n    s2_d100_cohens_d_llama: -0.85\n  result_rationale: 'In the median tier, Llama-3.2-1B shows the same directional pattern\n    as Qwen (ARC forgetting, GSM8K mild inflation) but with substantially reduced\n    magnitude. The γ sign is consistent (negative for ARC) but magnitude is roughly\n    half that of Qwen at comparable scale. This is expected: Llama-3.2-1B has different\n    architecture (GQA, different tokenizer, different pre-training data distribution)\n    which affects how SFT contamination interacts with stored knowledge. The rank\n    correlation (τ=0.60) is moderate — consistent direction but different magnitude\n    means the benchmarks don''t perfectly rank-order in susceptibility across families.\n\n    Critically, the γ CI for Llama alone does NOT exclude zero — this is the reduced\n    statistical strength characteristic of median tier. Only when pooled with Qwen\n    data (raising N toward 24) does the combined γ become borderline significant.\n    This means the multi-family finding is ''directionally consistent'' but not independently\n    confirmatory at the individual-family level.\n\n    S2 (Min-K++) shows a weaker reversal on Llama (d=-0.85 vs d=-1.98 on Qwen at 0.5B),\n    still negative but less extreme. This is consistent with the ''scale-dependent''\n    signal failure narrative: Llama-3.2-1B at 1B parameters is between 0.5B and 1.5B\n    Qwen in terms of effective capacity, so the reversal is attenuated. S1 remains\n    robust (AUC=0.94).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Cross-family contamination susceptibility'\n    columns:\n    - family\n    - scale\n    - arc_d100_delta_pp\n    - arc_gamma\n    - gamma_sign\n    - ci_excludes_zero\n    rows:\n    - - Qwen2.5\n      - 0.5B\n      - -17.6\n      - -1.21\n      - negative\n      - borderline\n    - - Qwen2.5\n      - 1.5B\n      - -0.3\n      - -0.18\n      - negative\n      - 'no'\n    - - Qwen2.5\n      - 3B\n      - 1.2\n      - -0.42\n      - negative\n      - 'no'\n    - - Llama-3.2\n      - 1B\n      - -5.5\n      - -0.75\n      - negative\n      - 'no'\n  - name: 'Table: Signal effectiveness cross-family'\n    columns:\n    - signal\n    - metric\n    - Qwen_0.5B\n    - Llama_1B\n    - consistent\n    rows:\n    - - S1 embedding\n      - d005 AUC\n      - 0.979\n      - 0.94\n      - 'yes'\n    - - S2 Min-K++\n      - d005 AUC\n      - 0.465\n      - 0.48\n      - yes (both fail)\n    - - S2 Min-K++\n      - d100 Cohen's d\n      - -1.98\n      - -0.85\n      - yes (both negative)\n    - - S4 Self-Critique\n      - d005 AUC\n      - 0.481\n      - 0.5\n      - yes (both fail)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Grouped bar chart: ARC accuracy delta (d100-d000) by model family and scale.\n    Shows Llama less susceptible than Qwen at comparable scale.'\n  - 'Scatter plot: Qwen γ_k vs Llama γ_k by benchmark, with identity line. Points\n    cluster in same quadrant but not on diagonal (consistent sign, different magnitude).'\n  analysis: 'Multi-family result confirms contamination susceptibility is partially\n    a benchmark property (ARC consistently shows forgetting) but magnitude is model-family-dependent.\n    This weakens the strongest form of the characterization claim (''contamination\n    is a benchmark property'') to the softer ''contamination direction is benchmark-determined\n    but magnitude is family-modulated.'' Kendall τ=0.60 provides moderate support.\n    The paper presents this honestly: ''directional consistency across families with\n    heterogeneous magnitude suggests contamination characterization requires per-family\n    calibration for quantitative use.''\n\n    '\n- name: rl_observational_case_study\n  plan_id: exp-009\n  description: 'Apply Self-Critique + available signals to existing RL-trained models:\n    Qwen2.5-Instruct (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B/7B. Fit IRT with\n    wider CIs. Expected regret quantification for model selection. Near-zero γ check\n    on known-clean benchmark (specificity test).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_models_evaluated: 8\n    s1_computable: true\n    s2_computable: true\n    s4_computable: true\n    s1_auc_instruct_d005_equivalent: 0.72\n    s2_instruct_mean_score: 1.69\n    s4_instruct_mean_score: -0.91\n    arc_gamma_observational: -0.52\n    arc_gamma_observational_ci:\n    - -1.8\n    - 0.8\n    arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    mmlu_gamma_observational: 0.18\n    mmlu_gamma_observational_ci:\n    - -0.9\n    - 1.3\n    gsm8k_gamma_observational: 0.35\n    gsm8k_gamma_observational_ci:\n    - -0.6\n    - 1.3\n    specificity_gamma: 0.05\n    specificity_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.7\n    - 0.8\n    specificity_passes: true\n    mean_ci_width_observational: 0.12\n    mean_ci_width_controlled: 0.07\n    ci_ratio: 1.7\n    regret_raw_top5: 0.015\n    regret_calibrated_top5: 0.018\n    regret_remove_rescore_top5: 0.012\n    calibration_improves_selection: false\n    deepseek_r1_arc_gamma_contribution: -0.3\n    deepseek_s1_score_elevated: true\n  result_rationale: 'In the median tier, the observational case study produces directional\n    but not statistically significant findings. Without ground-truth contamination\n    labels, the IRT fit relies entirely on Stage 1 signal scores as exposure proxies.\n    S1 (embedding similarity) on Instruct models is noisier than on controlled SFT\n    models because RL post-training alters the relationship between training data\n    exposure and embedding space proximity.\n\n    ARC γ is negative (consistent with controlled findings) but CI widely includes\n    zero — the observational setting lacks the statistical power of the controlled\n    experiment. This is expected: without dosage variation, all models cluster near\n    similar exposure levels, collapsing the range that IRT needs to estimate γ reliably.\n\n    Expected regret analysis shows calibration does NOT improve model selection in\n    this setting. Raw scores already rank models reasonably well (because contamination\n    effects at 1.5B+ are small per the scale-dependent finding). Remove-and-rescore\n    performs marginally better because it simply drops high-exposure items without\n    the risk of IRT miscalibration. This is an honest negative result that reinforces\n    the ''When NOT to calibrate'' message: in observational settings with no dosage\n    variation and large models, calibration overhead is not justified.\n\n    Specificity check passes: γ≈0 on TriviaQA (a benchmark not in training data) confirms\n    the framework doesn''t hallucinate contamination effects where none exist. This\n    is a valuable positive control.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Observational γ estimates (Instruct + DeepSeek models)'\n    columns:\n    - benchmark\n    - gamma\n    - ci_lower\n    - ci_upper\n    - ci_excludes_zero\n    - interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - ARC-Challenge\n      - -0.52\n      - -1.8\n      - 0.8\n      - 'no'\n      - Directionally consistent with controlled (forgetting), underpowered\n    - - MMLU\n      - 0.18\n      - -0.9\n      - 1.3\n      - 'no'\n      - Near-zero, consistent with scale-dependent attenuation\n    - - GSM8K\n      - 0.35\n      - -0.6\n      - 1.3\n      - 'no'\n      - Non-significant, consistent with gating rejection\n    - - TriviaQA (specificity)\n      - 0.05\n      - -0.7\n      - 0.8\n      - 'no'\n      - PASS — no false positive\n  - name: 'Table: Expected regret — top-5 model selection'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - regret_pp\n    - rank_tau_vs_clean\n    - ci_width\n    rows:\n    - - Raw scores\n      - 1.5\n      - 0.85\n      - N/A\n    - - ContamCal calibrated\n      - 1.8\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.12\n    - - Remove-and-rescore\n      - 1.2\n      - 0.88\n      - N/A\n    - - Linear regression\n      - 1.6\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.08\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Forest plot: γ estimates with 95% CIs for each benchmark in observational setting,\n    with controlled setting estimates overlaid as reference. Shows consistency in\n    direction but wider CIs.'\n  - 'Scatter: raw model rank vs calibrated model rank (top-5 highlighted), showing\n    minimal rank changes — calibration doesn''t improve selection.'\n  analysis: 'The observational case study is an honest demonstration of ContamCal''s\n    limitations in the wild. Without controlled dosage variation, the framework correctly\n    identifies directional effects but lacks power for quantitative calibration. This\n    supports the paper''s ''When NOT to calibrate'' section: observational settings\n    with large models (≥1.5B) and no dosage variation are below the identifiability\n    threshold. The specificity check is the key positive finding — validating that\n    the framework doesn''t produce false contamination diagnoses. Paper frames this\n    as ''ContamCal as diagnostic tool (is there contamination?) rather than corrective\n    tool (what''s the true score?) in observational settings.''\n\n    '\n- name: observational_72b\n  plan_id: exp-012\n  description: 'Qwen2.5-72B observational analysis (inference only, no SFT). Extends\n    ecological validity to frontier model scale.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    inference_gpu_hours: 12\n    mmlu_accuracy: 0.842\n    gsm8k_accuracy: 0.905\n    arc_challenge_accuracy: 0.945\n    humaneval_pass1: 0.685\n    s1_mean_exposure_mmlu: 0.42\n    s1_mean_exposure_arc: 0.38\n    s1_mean_exposure_gsm8k: 0.35\n    s2_minkpp_mean: 1.72\n    s4_selfcritique_mean: -0.88\n    contribution_to_pooled_n: 1\n    pooled_n_after_72b: 9\n    s1_72b_vs_3b_delta: 0.05\n    s1_72b_relative_rank: middle (not highest, not lowest)\n    position_bias_72b_mmlu: 0.02\n    position_bias_72b_arc: 0.015\n    position_bias_absent: true\n  result_rationale: 'In the median tier, 72B provides ecological validity but minimal\n    statistical contribution. A single model cannot independently estimate γ — it\n    only adds one data point to the pooled analysis. The 72B model''s near-ceiling\n    performance on ARC (94.5%) and strong performance on MMLU (84.2%) means contamination\n    effects, if any, are masked by the model''s genuine capability. This is the ''near-immunity''\n    regime taken to its extreme.\n\n    S1 exposure scores are moderate (not zero, because 72B''s training data likely\n    includes benchmark-adjacent content given its massive training corpus), but this\n    doesn''t imply contamination harm — it''s the expected background exposure level\n    for a frontier model. The key contribution is confirming that position bias (which\n    invalidated 0.5B MC data) is absent at 72B, validating that logprob-based MC evaluation\n    is reliable at frontier scale.\n\n    The finding supports the scale-dependent narrative without adding statistical\n    power. Paper uses 72B as a single sentence: ''Contamination effect at frontier\n    scale (72B) is undetectable, consistent with the three-regime taxonomy.''\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: 72B observational results'\n    columns:\n    - benchmark\n    - accuracy\n    - s1_mean_exposure\n    - interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU\n      - 0.842\n      - 0.42\n      - Strong performance, moderate background exposure\n    - - ARC-Challenge\n      - 0.945\n      - 0.38\n      - Near-ceiling, exposure ≠ inflation\n    - - GSM8K\n      - 0.905\n      - 0.35\n      - Strong, below ARC/MMLU exposure\n    - - HumanEval\n      - 0.685\n      - 0.28\n      - Lowest exposure (code is harder to memorize)\n  figures_description:\n  - Single point added to the scale vs contamination-effect plot (Figure 2 from exp-007).\n    72B sits firmly in the 'near-immunity' plateau.\n  analysis: '72B is appendix-level evidence. Its primary value is completeness (showing\n    the framework applies at frontier scale) and the position bias absence confirmation.\n    Does not change any statistical conclusions. In median tier, this experiment is\n    ''included for ecological validity'' — one sentence in the main text, details\n    in appendix.\n\n    '\n- name: closed_weight_2signal_mode\n  plan_id: exp-013\n  description: 'Degraded 2-signal mode (ConStat + Self-Critique, no logits/embeddings)\n    for closed-weight models. Ablation quantifying 2-signal vs 4-signal calibration\n    accuracy gap.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_signals: 2\n    s3_constat_d005_auc: 0.55\n    s4_selfcritique_d005_auc: 0.48\n    rrf_2signal_d005_auc: 0.56\n    rrf_4signal_d005_auc: 0.97\n    auc_degradation: 0.41\n    arc_gamma_2signal: -0.35\n    arc_gamma_2signal_ci:\n    - -2.5\n    - 1.8\n    arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    mae_2signal: 0.065\n    mae_4signal: 0.035\n    mae_raw: 0.059\n    coverage_95_2signal: 0.25\n    coverage_95_4signal: 0.35\n    calibration_improves_over_raw: false\n    mae_gap_2signal_vs_raw: 0.006\n    kendall_tau_2signal: 0.55\n    kendall_tau_4signal: 0.76\n  result_rationale: 'In the median tier, the 2-signal mode is essentially non-functional\n    for calibration. Both ConStat and Self-Critique showed AUC≈0.5 (chance level)\n    at 0.5B in exp-008. Without S1 (the only effective signal), the exposure scores\n    fed to IRT are near-random, making γ estimation impossible. The resulting γ CI\n    is extremely wide and includes zero — IRT cannot distinguish contaminated from\n    clean items when the input signal is noise.\n\n    Calibration MAE is WORSE than raw (0.065 vs 0.059) because the noisy exposure\n    scores introduce systematic error: IRT attempts to ''correct'' based on meaningless\n    signal variation, producing biased counterfactual estimates. This is the calibration\n    gating criterion in action: when input signal AUC < 0.55 (per exp-004 simulation),\n    calibration should not be attempted.\n\n    This result is presented honestly as a hard limitation: ContamCal requires model\n    weights (for S1 embedding similarity) to function. Closed-weight evaluation is\n    restricted to ConStat''s performance gap approach, which requires access to both\n    original and paraphrased items but not model internals — however, this alone is\n    insufficient for reliable calibration. Paper frames: ''closed-weight calibration\n    remains an open problem; ContamCal''s requirement for embedding access is a genuine\n    limitation, not a design choice that could be easily relaxed.''\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: 2-signal vs 4-signal calibration comparison'\n    columns:\n    - metric\n    - 2_signal\n    - 4_signal\n    - raw\n    - winner\n    rows:\n    - - Composite AUC\n      - 0.56\n      - 0.97\n      - N/A\n      - 4-signal\n    - - γ CI width\n      - 4.3\n      - 2.65\n      - N/A\n      - 4-signal\n    - - γ excludes zero\n      - 'no'\n      - borderline\n      - N/A\n      - 4-signal\n    - - MAE\n      - 0.065\n      - 0.035\n      - 0.059\n      - 4-signal\n    - - Coverage 95%\n      - 0.25\n      - 0.35\n      - N/A\n      - 4-signal\n    - - Kendall τ\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.82\n      - raw\n  - name: 'Table: Signal AUC by mode'\n    columns:\n    - signal\n    - available_closed_weight\n    - d005_auc\n    - contribution\n    rows:\n    - - S1 embedding\n      - no (requires weights)\n      - 0.979\n      - dominant\n    - - S2 Min-K++\n      - no (requires logits)\n      - 0.465\n      - none (chance)\n    - - S3 ConStat\n      - 'yes'\n      - 0.55\n      - marginal\n    - - S4 Self-Critique\n      - 'yes'\n      - 0.48\n      - none (chance)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Side-by-side calibration scatter plots: 2-signal (left, high scatter, regression\n    line far from identity) vs 4-signal (right, tighter, closer to identity).'\n  - ROC curves for 2-signal RRF vs 4-signal RRF, showing massive AUC gap.\n  analysis: 'The 2-signal mode is a confirmed failure mode that the paper presents\n    transparently. This strengthens the honest ''limitations'' section and supports\n    the broader narrative: contamination calibration fundamentally requires access\n    to model internals (at minimum, embedding space) because surface-level signals\n    (performance gaps, entropy patterns) lack discriminative power at the item level.\n    In median tier, this negative result is not surprising — it follows directly from\n    exp-008 showing S3/S4 at chance — but it''s the formal ablation needed for the\n    paper. Contributes to the ''When NOT to calibrate'' section: ''Do not attempt\n    calibration when (a) only closed-weight signals available, (b) composite signal\n    AUC < 0.60.'''\n","numLines":730,"startLine":1,"totalLines":730}}}
